{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Harass the Midianites, etc.</b> And He did not command to harass the Moabites, because it says (Numbers 25:1): ״וַיָּחֶל הָעָם לִזְנוֹת אֶל בְּנוֹת מוֹאָב״ — what is ״וַיָּחֶל״? Rather, this is its meaning: Israel were the ones who began and solicited the daughters of Moab, and they went to their tents and solicited the women and enticed them. Therefore it says ״וַיָּחֶל הָעָם״, for the people were the initiators and not the daughters of Moab. Therefore it says ״אֶל בְּנוֹת מוֹאָב״ and does not say ״עִם בְּנוֹת מוֹאָב״, but rather it comes to teach that Israel went to them; but Moab did not send their women and daughters to Israel. Therefore Moab was not held liable, for the one who begins the corruption is the principal party and upon him the sin depends." "But with the Midianites the matter was the reverse, for on the contrary — the Midianites made their daughters freely available to solicit Israel to prostitution. And reason dictates: how could Zimri's heart be filled to solicit a king's daughter? Rather, surely she solicited him. And even if they did not say so explicitly, at any rate they made the effort with a krater of wine to give them to drink a cup of venom in order to kindle in them the fire of passion, as is concluded in chapter Cheilek (Sanhedrin 106a). Therefore it says: ״צָרוֹר אֶת הַמִּדְיָנִים״, and why? ״כִּי צוֹרְרִים הֵם לָכֶם״ — that is to say, they came to you to bring you into the habit of this transgression, to harass to expose your nakedness; and not like the daughters of Moab, whom Israel came to. And it means: those harassers, the known ones, are yours — they began the corruption with you. And with this the word ״צוֹרְרִים״ is properly settled as a verb in the present tense; whereas according to our way, ״צוֹרְרִים״ is an epithet of theirs, and they are ״לָכֶם״ — that is, they came to you with instruments of cunning through giving them a krater of wine that habituates to immorality. And this is what it says: ״בְּנִכְלֵיהֶם אֲשֶׁר נִכְּלוּ לָכֶם עַל דְּבַר פְּעוֹר״, for the prostitution was intended to afflict them so that against their will they would return to their faith." "Another matter: ״וַיָּחֶל הָעָם״ — the language of profaneness, the opposite of holiness, for everywhere you find a boundary of immorality, there you find holiness (Leviticus Rabbah 24:6), and here they were occupied with immorality and profaned their holiness and became profane; therefore it says ״וַיָּחֶל״. And they learn from ״וַיָּחֵל נֹחַ אִישׁ הָאֲדָמָה וַיִּטַּע כָּרֶם״ (Genesis 9:20) that the ״וַיָּחֵל״ stated here too was by means of a krater of wine that habituated them to immorality. And some say that therefore ״וַיָּחֵל״ is stated because the beginning was with the daughters of Moab and the completion with the daughters of Ammon, and Scripture did not publicize them, just as it spared the younger one who tarried in prostitution and called her son's name Ben-Ammi — so too it did not publicize here. And this accords with the opinion of the Midrash (Sifrei, Ki Tetze 117) that states that Ammon and Moab, through causing Israel to sin, were distanced with an eternal distancing. But the plain sense of the verse stands against this Midrash to refute it, for behold it says (Deuteronomy 23:5): ״עַל דְּבַר אֲשֶׁר לֹא קִדְּמוּ אֶתְכֶם בַּלֶּחֶם וּבַמָּיִם״. And most of the commentators went out to search and did not find a sufficient resolution for this." "And I say that the matter is as its plain sense, and the one depends on the other — for it is not reasonable to distance two nations on account of the fact that they did not greet them with bread and water, but rather on account of their intention. For HaShem knew that the reason they did not greet them was so that they would be hungry and also thirsty from the exertion of the journey, and would be compelled to eat from the sacrifices of their gods and to drink their libation wine, which habituates to immorality. For such is the way of all who arrive from the road: they are tired and weary from the exertion of the journey, and they are hungry and thirsty, so they crave to eat and drink whatever comes to hand; they do not inspect between forbidden and permitted. And all their intention was to cause them to stumble in prostitution and thereby bring them to the deed of Peor. Therefore they did not greet them with bread, in order to starve them and feed them from the sacrifices of their gods, and they did not greet them with water, in order to give them to drink a cup of venom and their libation wine — and all this was through devices of faiths and cunning. And this explanation is precious; I do not know for what reason the commentators of our Torah, whose smallest is thicker than my waist, did not find it with their intellect."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:17.328171408Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}